 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Training time is high due to the need to optimize neural networks and probability models jointly [S1]. Inference time depends on the network depth and width; typically slower than fixed transform methods like DWT [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Training time is high due to the need to optimize neural networks and probability models jointly [S1]. Inference requires neural network forward passes, which are typically slower than fixed linear transforms [G].
 - **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing network parameters ($\theta_e, \theta_d, \phi$) in addition to the compressed bitstream. The bitstream size is determined by the learned rate $R(\hat{z}; \phi)$ [S1].
 - **Measured Performance:** On volumes up to $512^3$ (NYX cosmology), Sliced-Wasserstein Autoencoder Compression achieved 100–800% higher compression ratios than SZ2.1 and ZFP at matched PSNR [S2, unofficial]. However, throughput was 12–68 MB/s, significantly lower than traditional pipelines [S2, unofficial].
 - **Strengths:** Can achieve higher compression ratios for specific data distributions by learning non-linear features and adaptive probability models [S1]. Can incorporate perceptual metrics (e.g., Wasserstein Distance) for better visual quality [S3, unofficial].
 - **Limitations:** High computational cost for training and inference. Less generalizable than fixed transforms unless trained on diverse data. Throughput can be an order of magnitude lower than optimized traditional codecs [S2, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When high compression ratios are critical and computational resources allow for neural network inference, or when perceptual quality is more important than exact numerical fidelity [S1][S3, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use:** When real-time throughput is required, or when hardware does not support efficient neural network inference [S2, unofficial].
