 ## Background
-Learned transform coding intersects with the surface, as well as the offsets of the intersection points for the valid edges, which are then used to form a triangular mesh [S1]. The concept of optimizing rate-distortion trade-offs via learned models has been applied in various domains, including image and video compression [S1]. The specific formulation using encoder-decoder pairs with learned priors for volume data has been explored in recent research, such as Deep Implicit Volume Compression [S1]. Variants integrating autoencoders into scientific volume compression frameworks, like AE-SZ, have been proposed to improve compression ratios for large-scale datasets [S2, unofficial]. Perceptual optimization using Wasserstein Distance and common randomness has been applied to image codecs, showing potential for volume rendering applications [S3, unofficial].
+Learned transform coding addresses high-dimensional data where direct rate-distortion optimization is intractable [S1]. The concept of optimizing rate-distortion trade-offs via learned models has been applied in various domains, including image and video compression [S1]. The specific formulation using encoder-decoder pairs with learned priors for volume data has been explored in recent research, such as Deep Implicit Volume Compression [S1]. Variants integrating autoencoders into scientific volume compression frameworks, like AE-SZ, have been proposed to improve compression ratios for large-scale datasets [S2, unofficial]. Perceptual optimization using Wasserstein Distance and common randomness has been applied to image codecs, showing potential for volume rendering applications [S3, unofficial].
 
